## 1. Study Area

This study was conducted in five coffee-producing districts of the Western Hararghe Zone in eastern Ethiopia:

| District | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| Boke | Major coffee-producing district |
| Dara Labu | Major coffee-producing district |
| Habro | Major coffee-producing district |
| Shanan Dhugoo | Major coffee-producing district |
| Tulo | Major coffee-producing district |

**Geographic coordinates:** Approximately 7°30′–9°30′ N latitude and 40°–41° E longitude.

**Elevation:** 1,500–2,300 m above sea level.

**Climate:** Subtropical highland, with reported annual air temperatures of 15–25 °C (standard 2-m air temperature) and mean annual rainfall of approximately 900–1,200 mm.

**Rainfall pattern:** Bimodal — main rainy season (June–September) and shorter rainy season (March–May).

**Farming systems:** Smallholder garden-based agroforestry, intercropping coffee with khat (*Catha edulis*), sorghum, maize, beans, and shade trees.

**Important note on temperature:** The temperature predictor used in this study is **NASA POWER near-surface skin temperature**, which is systematically warmer than standard 2-meter air temperature. The mean value in this dataset is 34.47 °C (range: 32.00–37.00 °C). This distinction is explicitly acknowledged throughout the manuscript.

---

## 2. Research Design

This study employed a quantitative research design to systematically analyze numerical data on coffee production, climatic variables, and soil properties for machine learning-based production prediction.

The workflow proceeds in **eight stages**:

| Stage | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| 1 | Research problem and prediction objective defined |
| 2 | Agricultural, soil, and climatic data collected from multiple sources |
| 3 | Data entry, cleaning, and quality audit (missing values, duplicates, unit harmonization, IQR-based outlier flagging without removal) |
| 4 | Exploratory data analysis (EDA) — distributions and pairwise correlations |
| 5 | Chronological train–test split |
| 6 | In-fold preprocessing (imputation, standardization, one-hot encoding) |
| 7 | Model training and hyperparameter tuning using five-fold expanding-window time-series cross-validation |
| 8 | Locked test-set evaluation followed by post-hoc diagnostics |

---

## 3. Data Collection

### 3.1 Data Sources

| Data Type | Source | Resolution |
|-----------|--------|------------|
| Agricultural records (production, cultivated area, fertilizer use) | District and zonal agricultural offices | District–year |
| Soil properties (pH, N, P, K, organic C, soil type) | Soil laboratory reports from district offices | District–year |
| Climate data (temperature, precipitation, humidity, wind, solar radiation, soil wetness) | Ethiopian National Meteorological Agency (ENMA) and NASA POWER | ≈0.5° × 0.625° grid |

### 3.2 Observation Period

- **Ethiopian Calendar:** 2000–2015 EC
- **Gregorian Calendar:** Approximately 2007/08–2022/23 GC
- **Data acquisition date:** 2023/24 academic year

**Important:** The observation period and the data acquisition date are distinct.

### 3.3 Data Harmonization

1. **Calendar conversion:** Climate observation dates were converted from Ethiopian Calendar to Gregorian Calendar and matched to corresponding agricultural years.

2. **Temporal aggregation:**
   - Annual means for temperature, humidity, wind speed, and solar radiation
   - Annual totals for precipitation

3. **Spatial assignment:**
   - Climate variables assigned to each district based on district centroid from NASA POWER grid
   - Soil variables assigned based on dominant soil type reported for each district-year record

4. **Unit harmonization:** All variables converted to standard units before analysis.

### 3.4 Dataset Structure

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| Total observations | N = 500 |
| Districts | 5 |
| Years | 16 (2000–2015 EC) |
| Unit of analysis | Record within a district–year (not district–year aggregate) |
| Records per district | 100 each (total across 16 years) |
| Records per district–year | Ranged from 0 to 13 |
| Year-level record counts | Ranged from 18 (2009) to 48 (2015) |

**Note:** The dataset does not contain 80 district–year aggregates. The structure reflects the available secondary data, which included multiple production records per district–year from different recording units.

