## 6. Chronological Train–Test Split

### 6.1 Split Design

| Set | Period | N | Percentage |
|-----|--------|---|------------|
| Training | 2000–2012 EC | 389 | 77.8% |
| Test | 2013–2015 EC | 111 | 22.2% |

### 6.2 Rationale

- A **chronological split** respects temporal ordering and prevents data leakage.
- A random split could place future years in the training set and earlier years in the test set, inflating performance.
- The test set was **locked** and consulted only once after model selection was complete.

### 6.3 Random Seed

All random operations used **random seed = 42** for reproducibility.

---

## 7. In-Fold Preprocessing

### 7.1 Preprocessing Steps

| Step | Method | Fitted Where |
|------|--------|--------------|
| Missing numerical values | Median imputation | Inside training folds |
| Missing categorical values | Mode imputation | Inside training folds |
| Numerical standardization | StandardScaler | Inside training folds |
| Categorical encoding | OneHotEncoder | Inside training folds |

### 7.2 Leakage Prevention

- All preprocessing was implemented using scikit-learn **Pipelines** and **ColumnTransformers**.
- Preprocessing was fitted **only on training data** within each cross-validation fold.
- The test set was never used during preprocessing, tuning, or model selection.

### 7.3 Outlier Treatment

- IQR flagging was performed during the data audit (not during modeling).
- No outlier removal was applied during modeling to preserve natural variability.
- Imputation occurred only inside the cross-validation pipeline.

